# The preset policy in kgsm-base enables this unit; applying it is symlink manipulation on disk, so
# it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Loading BPF is the unit's own job, and
# starting it is the node's post-transaction hook's, which is where a running system is available.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled this unit keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-net-meter.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  The network meter attaches to /sys/fs/cgroup/kgsm.slice, so kgsm-watchdog has to have created that
  slice first. The unit is ordered after the watchdog and wanted by it, so it attaches on the
  watchdog's next start and re-attaches on every one after that.

  Until it runs, kgsm-monitor serves host metrics normally and reports per-server rxBps/txBps as
  null — "not measured", which is the honest answer, never a fabricated 0.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  The meter's programs are already loaded in the kernel; the new object takes effect on the next
  restart of kgsm-net-meter.service, which the node applies for you.

MSG
}
